WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.434 --> 00:00:02.768
-Our next guest is the
best-selling author of books

00:00:02.768 --> 00:00:05.372
such as "A Gentleman in Moscow"
and "Rules of Civility."

00:00:05.372 --> 00:00:06.906
His latest novel,
"The Lincoln Highway,"

00:00:06.906 --> 00:00:08.207
is on sale now.

00:00:08.207 --> 00:00:10.643
Please welcome to the show,
Amor Towles, everyone!

00:00:10.643 --> 00:00:12.645
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:12.645 --> 00:00:21.454 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:21.454 --> 00:00:24.057
Amor, I'm so happy you're here.
-It's great to be here.

00:00:24.057 --> 00:00:25.625
-I was very excited
about this book.

00:00:25.625 --> 00:00:27.326
"Gentleman in Moscow"
was one of my favorite books

00:00:27.326 --> 00:00:29.329
of the last decade.
[ Cheers ]

00:00:29.329 --> 00:00:31.464
We have some --
And I did represent.

00:00:31.464 --> 00:00:33.199
Back when I was doing
shows in the attic --

00:00:33.199 --> 00:00:34.667
there's "The Gentleman
in Moscow" right there.

00:00:34.667 --> 00:00:38.404
I was trying to support authors
by doing a very small thing.

00:00:38.404 --> 00:00:39.605
[ Laughter ]

00:00:39.605 --> 00:00:41.374
Did it make a giant difference
in your sales?

00:00:41.374 --> 00:00:43.042
-We miss the Captain.
-You miss the Sea Captain.

00:00:43.042 --> 00:00:44.244
-We miss the Sea Captain.
-Thank you very much.

00:00:44.244 --> 00:00:46.579
Now, I actually --
I do a thing every week

00:00:46.579 --> 00:00:48.348
called "Corrections,"
where I address

00:00:48.348 --> 00:00:49.883
the YouTube comments
about things I get wrong.

00:00:49.883 --> 00:00:52.751
You have hyper-specific
historical details

00:00:52.751 --> 00:00:54.887
in your books, and you,
to some degree,

00:00:54.887 --> 00:00:57.757
also engage with people
who tell you what you got wrong.

00:00:57.757 --> 00:00:59.792
-Yes, this is the great thing
about the modern era

00:00:59.792 --> 00:01:02.561
is that if you go to my website,
you can reach me directly.

00:01:02.561 --> 00:01:04.430
-Yeah.
-And so, we get corrections,

00:01:04.430 --> 00:01:06.565
authors get corrections
right off the bat.

00:01:06.565 --> 00:01:10.436
And probably within 72 hours
of this book being released,

00:01:10.436 --> 00:01:12.105
the first corrections
started coming in.

00:01:12.105 --> 00:01:14.140
-Wow.
-And in the book,

00:01:14.140 --> 00:01:16.709
one of the characters,
while he's in high school,

00:01:16.709 --> 00:01:19.345
tries to burn his thesaurus
on a football field

00:01:19.345 --> 00:01:22.849
and, by mistake, the goal post
catches on fire.

00:01:22.849 --> 00:01:25.351
And he admires it, as the fire
goes up the middle post

00:01:25.351 --> 00:01:27.220
and up the arms
in sort of this beautiful

00:01:27.220 --> 00:01:31.257
standing-man burning figure.
And so, within 72 hours,

00:01:31.257 --> 00:01:34.760
an e-mail comes in, saying,
"I really enjoyed your book,

00:01:34.760 --> 00:01:38.898
but you should know that, in
1954, when the book takes place,

00:01:38.898 --> 00:01:40.666
that's not
what a goal post looked like

00:01:40.666 --> 00:01:42.634
on a football field."
[ Laughter ]

00:01:42.634 --> 00:01:45.338
It was an 'H,' not a 'Y.'

00:01:45.338 --> 00:01:48.741
But I was not an athlete
in high school.

00:01:48.741 --> 00:01:51.978
And so, to fulfill
my athletic responsibility,

00:01:51.978 --> 00:01:55.114
I was actually the manager of
the varsity girls soccer team.

00:01:55.114 --> 00:01:57.049
-Okay.
-You know, which in retrospect,

00:01:57.049 --> 00:01:59.251
was the right job.
-Yeah.

00:01:59.251 --> 00:02:02.588
-That's a good interest.
But, so, I was not an athlete,

00:02:02.588 --> 00:02:05.859
but so, there's some pleasure
to know that football fans

00:02:05.859 --> 00:02:08.394
are reading my novel.
-That's right.

00:02:08.394 --> 00:02:10.396
Certainly football fans
from 1954.

00:02:10.396 --> 00:02:12.498
-And they're persnickety,
but they're good, you know.

00:02:12.498 --> 00:02:15.134
-Now, will you -- obviously,
you can't change a book

00:02:15.134 --> 00:02:16.969
once it's written.
-That's right.

00:02:16.969 --> 00:02:18.404
-And it's gonna forever
be a "Y."

00:02:18.404 --> 00:02:21.674
-No, when I go from hardcover
to paperback,

00:02:21.674 --> 00:02:24.277
I will tend to make about
25 or 30 changes.

00:02:24.277 --> 00:02:26.278
-Really? All based
on historical accuracy?

00:02:26.278 --> 00:02:30.149
-Well, or based
on sort of input from readers.

00:02:30.149 --> 00:02:33.052
There is a smaller scene
in the book

00:02:33.052 --> 00:02:38.691
where at sort of a circus
of sorts where a woman --

00:02:38.691 --> 00:02:40.760
a large woman rides out
on a tricycle and a tutu,

00:02:40.760 --> 00:02:45.131
and she's chased by two seals
dressed as police officers.

00:02:45.131 --> 00:02:47.533
And same thing.
I got an e-mail very quickly

00:02:47.533 --> 00:02:50.936
from someone saying,
"I liked your book,

00:02:50.936 --> 00:02:53.105
but as someone who's been
a marine biologist

00:02:53.105 --> 00:02:59.012
for 50 years, I have to tell you
that no seal would do that

00:02:59.012 --> 00:03:02.815
because seals do not cavort.

00:03:02.815 --> 00:03:05.317
Sea lions cavort."
[ Laughter ]

00:03:05.317 --> 00:03:07.119
So, yes,
when we go to paperback,

00:03:07.119 --> 00:03:09.889
I may consider going from seals
to sea lions in that scene.

00:03:09.889 --> 00:03:12.291
That's fantastic, and I think
that's the kind of engagement

00:03:12.291 --> 00:03:15.962
with fans
that has a positive outcome.

00:03:15.962 --> 00:03:18.831
And another thing
that you do for your fans --

00:03:18.831 --> 00:03:20.400
And I don't know
if you set out to do this,

00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:24.904
but there are some Easter eggs
in that this book has some dates

00:03:24.904 --> 00:03:26.806
that do line up
with "Gentleman of Moscow"

00:03:26.806 --> 00:03:28.841
even though they take place
in two different places,

00:03:28.841 --> 00:03:30.376
and the characters
never interact or meet.

00:03:30.376 --> 00:03:32.979
-Yeah. So, this story
takes place over 10 days

00:03:32.979 --> 00:03:35.081
in 1954, and it's about,
you know,

00:03:35.081 --> 00:03:37.349
three 18-year-old boys
and an 8-year-old boy

00:03:37.349 --> 00:03:39.618
going from Nebraska
to New York City.

00:03:39.618 --> 00:03:42.722
And as I was finishing
the first draft of the book,

00:03:42.722 --> 00:03:45.391
it suddenly occurred to me
that this book,

00:03:45.391 --> 00:03:49.328
which ends in June 1954,
that my last book,

00:03:49.328 --> 00:03:52.332
"A Gentleman in Moscow,"
also ended in June 1954.

00:03:52.332 --> 00:03:55.334
But that's a 30-year saga
set in Moscow.

00:03:55.334 --> 00:03:57.470
But so, as I sort of began
to dwell on that,

00:03:57.470 --> 00:04:00.072
I tinkered with the timeline
a little bit in my first draft,

00:04:00.072 --> 00:04:03.842
and now "A Gentleman in Moscow,"
the culminating moment

00:04:03.842 --> 00:04:07.313
of the entire novel
occurs at midnight on June 1954

00:04:07.313 --> 00:04:10.817
where all of the telephones
in the Metropol Hotel go off,

00:04:10.817 --> 00:04:13.419
start ringing, and events occur.

00:04:13.419 --> 00:04:15.454
So now this book,
the culminating moment

00:04:15.454 --> 00:04:17.990
at the end of this book
occurs at 5:00 p.m.

00:04:17.990 --> 00:04:21.727
on the 21st of June, 1954.
And given the time change,

00:04:21.727 --> 00:04:24.697
it means that the two books
end at the exact same time,

00:04:24.697 --> 00:04:27.533
you know, in history.
-That's really cool,

00:04:27.533 --> 00:04:31.270
and that's a severe case of OCD.
[ Applause ]

00:04:31.270 --> 00:04:33.306
-Yeah, that's right.
-Severe OCD. Yeah.

00:04:33.306 --> 00:04:36.475
-Yeah.
-I would imagine --

00:04:36.475 --> 00:04:38.911
I was lucky enough
to meet you and your lovely wife

00:04:38.911 --> 00:04:40.379
a couple summers ago.

00:04:40.379 --> 00:04:42.982
And I know for a fact that you
let her read your manuscripts.

00:04:42.982 --> 00:04:44.817
And I'm wondering --
you have kids --

00:04:44.817 --> 00:04:46.452
are your books
too adult for them,

00:04:46.452 --> 00:04:49.521
or have they found their ways
to them, as well?

00:04:49.521 --> 00:04:50.856
-We have two teenagers.
-Okay.

00:04:50.856 --> 00:04:53.192
-And so,
they're certainly old enough.

00:04:53.192 --> 00:04:55.428
But, you know, teenagers,
they're mostly there

00:04:55.428 --> 00:04:57.196
to keep you in your place.
You know what I mean?

00:04:57.196 --> 00:04:59.932
They don't spend a lot of time
admiring what you're doing.

00:04:59.932 --> 00:05:01.967
[ Laughter ]

00:05:01.967 --> 00:05:04.604
But so my wife is one of
my first readers,

00:05:04.604 --> 00:05:07.206
and when the manuscript
was done and I gave it to her,

00:05:07.206 --> 00:05:09.842
she likes to read in bed,
but she will not

00:05:09.842 --> 00:05:11.411
read my work in bed,
which is nice,

00:05:11.411 --> 00:05:12.745
because she falls asleep
when she reads.

00:05:12.745 --> 00:05:14.680
-Right.
That is very nice.

00:05:14.680 --> 00:05:16.849
-Yeah, and that's very intense
for the writer of his new book

00:05:16.849 --> 00:05:19.285
to look over,
find your wife asleep.

00:05:19.285 --> 00:05:22.588
So, she would read in the
evening and leave it downstairs,

00:05:22.588 --> 00:05:27.560
and it turned out that my son,
Stokley, who at the age of 18,

00:05:27.560 --> 00:05:30.530
was coming downstairs
after we were asleep

00:05:30.530 --> 00:05:34.000
and reading the book kind of
by himself in secret at night.

00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:36.002
-That's great.
-Which is a great compliment

00:05:36.002 --> 00:05:38.004
from a teenager.
-But did he let you know

00:05:38.004 --> 00:05:39.538
that he was doing that
when he was done?

00:05:39.538 --> 00:05:40.840
Did he reveal?

00:05:40.840 --> 00:05:42.608
-Well, he was very
reserved about it.

00:05:42.608 --> 00:05:43.709
He was probably
one of the people

00:05:43.709 --> 00:05:45.244
sending me e-mails
with corrections.

00:05:45.244 --> 00:05:46.379
[ Laughter ]

00:05:46.379 --> 00:05:47.880
-I like that --
Like, I would imagine,

00:05:47.880 --> 00:05:50.048
when my kids are teenagers,
based on how they are now,

00:05:50.048 --> 00:05:51.650
they would say, like, "You know,
I just want you to know

00:05:51.650 --> 00:05:54.020
I read your book in secret."
And I would say, "And?"

00:05:54.020 --> 00:05:55.888
And they're like,
"And I -- I finished it."

00:05:55.888 --> 00:05:59.258
-Yeah, "I finished it."
It's another victory.

00:05:59.258 --> 00:06:01.493
-Another victory.
Congratulations.

00:06:01.493 --> 00:06:03.028
I'm so excited to read it.

00:06:03.028 --> 00:06:04.596
And it's just a delight
to have you here.

00:06:04.596 --> 00:06:05.798
Thank you so much.
-Thanks for having me.

00:06:05.798 --> 00:06:08.100
[ Cheers and applause ]
-Amor Towles, everybody!

00:06:08.100 --> 00:06:10.870
"The Lincoln Highway" is on sale
now wherever books are sold,

00:06:10.870 --> 00:06:13.039
but please support local
and independent bookstores.

